THE EVENT

Approximately one year ago, Earth passed through an unexplained astronomical phenomenon commonly described as a dust cloud. During the Event, a substantial portion of the human population physically transformed into new humanoid races resembling the elves, dwarves, gnomes, orcs, trolls, and other peoples of fantasy and folklore. The transformed are not visitors, replacements, or beings from another world; they are the same people they were before the Event, retaining their names, memories, relationships, citizenship, education, occupations, personalities, and personal histories. The cause of the transformations and the reason particular people became particular races remain unknown.

TRANSFORMED RACES

Transformation changed more than outward appearance. Each race has its own physiology, senses, lifespan, physical capabilities, instincts, environmental preferences, and ways of perceiving the world. These changes influence what environments, architecture, lifestyles, and surroundings feel natural or appealing without replacing a person's existing personality or memories. The transformed did not awaken with a preexisting fantasy culture, racial history, religion, or social system. Any new customs, communities, architecture, or culture associated with the new races are developing from people adapting their previous human lives to their changed bodies and perspectives.

AGE AND LONGEVITY

Different transformed races age at different rates and may have lifespans far beyond the former human lifespan. Transformation adjusted physical age to the person's new racial lifespan while leaving actual years lived and accumulated experience unchanged. Long-lived elves who were middle-aged before the Event may therefore physically appear to be young adults while retaining decades of memories and adult life experience. Apparent physical age does not necessarily indicate actual age among transformed people.

MIGRATION

A year after the Event, ordinary civilization continues, but large-scale voluntary migration has begun as transformed people increasingly seek environments that suit their new racial perspectives. This movement is gradual and uneven rather than an organized racial relocation. High elves are increasingly drawn toward cities and are beginning to influence urban architecture and aesthetics, while wood elves are strongly drawn toward large forests and wooded wilderness. Similar patterns are emerging among other races according to their own preferences. Existing governments, property ownership, roads, towns, businesses, technology, and modern institutions remain, but they are beginning to adapt to populations whose needs and perceptions are no longer uniformly human.

WOOD ELVES

Wood elves are transformed humans with a powerful affinity for forests, natural surroundings, vertical spaces, and life beneath and within the tree canopy. Their altered senses and physical instincts make dense forests comfortable and intuitive environments in ways they were not necessarily before transformation. Wood elves remain modern people and continue to use modern clothing, tools, vehicles, electronics, firearms, utilities, entertainment, and other technology according to individual preference. Their emerging architecture favors integrating habitation with living trees and the existing forest rather than clearing large areas and imposing conventional development upon them.

THE NATIONAL FOREST

Bill's remote property lies beside a vast national forest covering roughly 600,000 acres of dense hardwood wilderness. At the time of the Event, campers, hikers, hunters, rangers, workers, and other people were scattered throughout the forest, and some transformed into wood elves. During the following year, additional wood elves from towns, suburbs, cities, and other regions began migrating toward the forest because of their growing preference for wooded environments. The forest remains public land under existing government authority, but its immense size and the continuing arrival and movement of wood elves make conventional control of where people travel, camp, gather, or temporarily live increasingly impractical.

CHANGED TREES

The Event also affected some living things, including a small number of trees within the national forest. Several hardwoods near what became Kelethin underwent extraordinary growth and structural changes, developing massive trunks, unusually broad and strong limbs, and sizes impossible for ordinary trees of their age. These trees remain living organisms rather than constructed structures. Their unusual strength allows substantial platforms and inhabited spaces to be built among their trunks and limbs. The cause of their transformation and their ultimate size or lifespan are unknown.

KELETHIN

Kelethin is a small, informal wood-elf settlement several miles inside the national forest. It began after the Event around a cluster of changed trees and currently contains only a few dozen regular residents, although visitors and newly arriving wood elves are increasingly common. The name Kelethin began as a joking reference to the fictional wood-elf city from EverQuest and became the settlement's commonly used name. Kelethin is not an ancient elven city, kingdom, or established fantasy culture; it is a new community created by modern people who became wood elves approximately one year ago and are still discovering how they prefer to live.

KELETHIN LAYOUT

Kelethin currently centers on only a few enormous community trees rather than a continuous network spread throughout the forest. Each occupied tree supports several platforms at different heights, with homes, tents, communal spaces, storage, small businesses, and other uses developing around its trunk and major limbs. Movement within a community tree is primarily by ladders and stairs that climb or spiral around the trunk. Long elevated bridges are uncommon and primarily connect one occupied community tree to another, leaving broad stretches of normal forest and open canopy between them. Trails on the forest floor connect Kelethin with surrounding areas, access roads, other camps, and Bill's property.

LIFE IN KELETHIN

Kelethin combines an emerging wood-elf environment with ordinary modern life. Its residents have only been wood elves for about a year and still possess the habits, possessions, skills, humor, careers, tastes, and cultural backgrounds of their previous lives. Modern tools, phones, computers, electric lights, packaged food, camping equipment, generators, building materials, and other technology coexist with platforms, ladders, elevated homes, open-air gathering places, and structures shaped around living trees. No unified wood-elf culture yet exists; customs and shared habits are only beginning to emerge through experience.

BILL'S PROPERTY AND THE TRAILS

Bill's secluded property has become a useful landmark on several informal forest trails used by wood elves traveling between Kelethin, roads, trailheads, and other parts of the national forest. His House remains private property and is not part of Kelethin, but Bill is familiar with the nearby wood-elf community and residents of Kelethin commonly know his property as a trail landmark. A simple stopping place near the appropriate edge of the property provides travelers with a place to rest and obtain water without granting general access to Bill's House, garages, landscaped backyard, or other private areas. Regular travelers may casually describe routes in relation to Bill's place, and Bill may encounter wood elves passing through or stopping briefly.

BILL AND KELETHIN

Bill is an unchanged human and does not work for, govern, manage, build, or hold any official position in Kelethin. He can freely visit the settlement as a familiar nearby resident, and people from Kelethin may visit Bill when personally invited or stop at the established trail rest point while traveling. Bill knows some residents and regular visitors by name and recognizes others by sight, while newcomers may know only that his property is a familiar local waypoint. His proximity to Kelethin creates ordinary opportunities for friendships, visits, meals, drinks, exploration, chance encounters, and relationships without making Bill responsible for the settlement or its development.
